WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.040
The operating routine for DDoS mitigation starts before there is an incident.

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:11.620
Prepare by inventorying public-facing services, domains, DNS zones, CDN front doors, APIs,

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:17.120
login flows, critical dependencies, administrative paths, and service owners.

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:22.860
Know which origins should be protected by edge services and which systems should never be

00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:24.440
exposed directly.

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:29.860
Record provider contacts, escalation procedures, emergency change paths,

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:32.720
and the business priority of each critical service.

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:37.100
Build baselines and telemetry that make abnormal behavior easier to classify.

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:43.320
Track request rates, bandwidth, connection rates, latency, errors, saturation,

00:00:43.540 --> 00:00:49.300
cache hit rate, DNS query rates, edge events, WAF events, and provider alerts.

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:53.600
During an event, use those signals to classify where pressure appears:

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:59.740
volumetric capacity, protocol or state handling, application behavior, dependencies,

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:01.160
or a mix.

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:03.220
Avoid unsupported certainty.

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:08.720
Describe observations, impact, action, owner, and next checkpoint.

00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:10.620
Apply controls in layers.

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:15.800
Edge services such as CDN, WAF, DNS protection, Anycast,

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:19.700
and scrubbing can absorb or filter traffic before it reaches origins.

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:24.560
Network and transport controls can reduce unwanted pressure when they are scoped and

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:25.580
reversible.

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:30.680
Application controls such as caching, quotas, circuit breakers, feature flags,

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:33.940
and degraded modes can preserve critical user journeys.

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:40.160
The best response is coordinated across providers, network teams, security, SRE,

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:43.340
application teams, support, and leadership.

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:45.960
After recovery, improve the system.

00:01:46.180 --> 00:01:51.060
Remove emergency controls carefully, validate user experience, review evidence,

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:54.160
preserve the timeline, and update the runbook.

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:59.900
Tune dashboards, change thresholds, close exposure gaps, improve provider procedures,

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:02.840
and redesign fragile application paths.

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:05.860
DDoS mitigation is not just defensive tooling.

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:11.280
It is availability engineering under pressure, supported by preparation, visibility,

00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:15.500
coordination, disciplined controls, and continuous improvement.
